From d6f66dfb8830ac8871f0a2682ce879e21768c586 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 14:30:23 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] security: remediate the 2026-08-26 audit - 2 critical, 8 high

A six-agent review of the Reaper-authored surface (15 new C files plus the
deltas in web.c, services.c, qos.c and firewall.c, measured against the
3006.102.8 base) produced 2 Critical and 8 High findings. All ten are fixed
here. Every one carries a proven reachability trace - nothing speculative was
changed - and a recurring shape runs through the severe ones: the mitigation
already existed and was one step short of covering its own stated threat.

C1  rc/reaper_fw.c - turning the firewall OFF re-applied it, permanently.
    rfw_gen_apply()'s teardown branch wrote apply.sh.tmp and returned success
    without ever renaming it into place; the enabled path does exactly that at
    its own exit. stop_reaper_fw() then chmod+exec'd the PREVIOUS, still-enabled
    script - rebuilding and re-hooking every chain it had been asked to remove -
    and unlinked it afterwards. Net: engine enforcing, UI reporting it off, and
    no script left that could take it down until a reboot. If the ruleset being
    disabled was the one that locked you out, the escape hatch was gone.
    Introduced by ea085cdf16 (v2.5.1). The tell on a live box was an
    accumulating /tmp/reaper_fw/apply.sh.tmp.

C2  rc/gatekeeper.c + httpd/web.c - blocking your own device was a one-click,
    unrecoverable lockout. Neither Gatekeeper CGI compared the target MAC
    against the requesting client, though httpd already exports it. The v1.7.3
    $GKADMIN escape hatch was appended in the quarantine TAIL - after the
    per-MAC DROP, and not emitted at all when gk_default=1 - so the DROP won in
    every mode. REAPER_GKI sits at INPUT position 1, above the stock lo ACCEPT,
    so a self-block severed the admin UI, SSH and LAN together, with the captive
    page unreachable too: no in-band recovery. The hatch now goes in FIRST,
    ahead of the per-device rules, and both CGIs refuse a self-block.

H1  rmcpd/rmcpd.c - the slow-read guard did not cover its own documented
    threat. The header loop's deadline was only tested once fgets returned a
    COMPLETE line, so bytes dribbled inside one line never tripped it, and the
    request line was read before the deadline existed at all. serve_conn() runs
    inline from the accept loop, so a parked connection also stopped the
    session-expiry and USB-key-removal checks - which run only BETWEEN
    connections - from ever firing, outliving both revocation paths. Bounded now
    with SIGALRM installed without SA_RESTART, which covers the TLS path too.

H2  rc/qos.c + rc/reaper_pbr.c - a QoS rebuild deleted policy routing, and it
    failed open. del_iQosRules() is `iptables -t mangle -F` with no chain
    argument and add_qos_rules() restores the table without -n, so REAPER_PBR
    and its PREROUTING jump were removed outright. The ip rule entries survive
    that, so nothing marked packets any more and every selected flow fell
    through to main: traffic that should have been tunnelled left over the WAN
    in clear and BLOCK rules stopped blocking. New reaper_pbr_reassert() is
    called from the add_iQosRules tail and both HW-QoS teardown returns.

H3  shared/reaper_jfile.c - rjf_set() created only the last directory level, so
    with the firewall engine off (the shipped default, and the natural order of
    work) /tmp/reaper_fw did not exist and every list save failed ENOENT. The
    same sink stages an .rbk restore, so the firewall half was silently counted
    as rejected while the banner reported success.

H4  shared/+rc/+httpd/+www - rwarden_ban/allow/cfeeds were the last Reaper lists
    still in nvram and hit the same silent 1 KB kernel cap the device, routing
    and firewall lists did: the list topped out near 63 CIDRs and the next entry
    no-opped while the UI reported a successful save. They now live in
    /jffs/rwarden via the shared store, with a marker-guarded one-shot migration
    that only unsets nvram after all three files and the marker are written.
    rw_list_get() returns a caller-owned buffer rather than rjf_get()'s shared
    static, because allow and ban are resolved in one expression. The page could
    no longer ride the nvram apply form, so it POSTs the lists to
    reaper_warden.cgi?action=save_lists and waits for that reply before
    submitting - submitting first would restart rwarden against the old lists.
    Settings export and import both route through one predicate so they cannot
    drift apart.

H5  gkd/gkd.c - the AiMesh onboarding window asserted a bare `-j RETURN` at
    position 1 of REAPER_GKI, above the cfg_relist exemptions and every
    per-device rule, so quarantined AND explicitly blocked devices regained full
    access to the router. It opened on cfg_obstatus=2, which is the ordinary
    AiMesh SEARCH an admin runs from Network Map, not a join. Now limited to
    REQ/LOCKED and capped by a local deadline, since cfg_obstatus is written by
    the closed cfg_server and has no defaults entry to fall back to.

H6  gkd/gkd.c - the self-heal backoff was structurally dead: the throttle was a
    process-local static and the action it throttled, notify_rc("restart_gk"),
    kills and respawns this gkd, so the replacement started with the counter at
    zero and the 120 s guard was true again on its first tick. A persistent MISS
    (a disabled-but-addressed SDN bridge, or an addon-created br-*) meant an
    unbounded rebuild plus fc flush every 10-30 s for the life of the box. The
    throttle now lives in tmpfs and survives the respawn, with a strike limit.

H7  httpd/web.c + rc/rexport.c - one click on Test could hang the web UI until a
    reboot. doSystem() ran the exporter synchronously and httpd is a
    single-process select loop; the syslog engine pipes into openssl s_client
    with no timeout, and -quiet implies -ign_eof, so against a collector that
    holds the session open it never returned. Now detached with a bounded wait,
    and s_client exits on EOF.

H8  rtrafd/rtrafd.c - poll_conntrack() had no line cap and, once every slot held
    a non-zero key, each miss walked all 16384 entries. Bounding the probe was
    not an option: abandoning a chain early makes the fl->key == key test fail
    and credits a flow's whole cumulative counter as a single delta. Added an
    amortised compaction that rebuilds the table when a probe runs long,
    restoring the empty slots that terminate a chain without touching lookup
    semantics.

Reviewed against the prior audits so nothing already refuted was re-raised.
Verified: full rt-be96u build green (MAKE_EXIT=0), and the H4 store confirmed
present in the staged filesystem rather than inferred from the compile.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
